    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "retenir",
     "title": "Négation et question",
     "md": "- Négation : **do not** (don't) / **does not** (doesn't) + verbe sans s : She **does not** go to school on Sunday.\n- Question : **Do** you get up early ? — **Does** he play football ?\n\nAprès does / doesn't, le verbe reste à l'infinitif : « Does he **play** ? » (pas « plays »)."
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Exemple 1 : ajouter -s ou -es",
     "statement": "Mets à la 3e personne : « I go to school. I watch TV. I study. »",
     "steps": [
      {
       "md": "go → he **goes** (verbe en -o : -es)."
      },
      {
       "md": "watch → he **watches** (ch : -es)."
      },
      {
       "md": "study → he **studies** (consonne + y : -ies)."
      }
     ],
     "answer": "**He goes to school. He watches TV. He studies.**"
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Exemple 2 : la négation",
     "statement": "Mets à la forme négative : « Mballa plays football on Monday. »",
     "steps": [
      {
       "md": "Sujet « Mballa » = he : on emploie **does not**."
      },
      {
       "md": "Le verbe perd son -s : **play**."
      }
     ],
     "answer": "**Mballa does not play football on Monday.**"
    },
    {
     "type": "audio",
     "lang": "en-GB",
     "text": "I get up at six. I have breakfast at seven. I go to school at half past seven."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "pieges",
     "title": "Erreurs fréquentes",
     "md": "- Oublier le -s : « He go » ; il faut **He goes**.\n- Ajouter -s après does : « Does he plays ? » est faux.\n- Dire « I am go to school » : **I go**.\n- Écrire « studys » : **studies**.\n- Confondre **do** (I, you, we, they) et **does** (he, she, it)."
    },
